b/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167762 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "8ed0465c-f38c-56a2-acbf-cf8b05298867" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167762 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168131 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167763 #edb/ref 1058 #edb/ref 17592186167667 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167763 #edb/ref 1061 37402 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167763 #edb/ref 1062 #uuid "0fec3647-5057-5207-8619-9dd7001d1b85" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186167763 #edb/ref 1063 #edb/ref 17592186168132 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168075 #edb/ref 1064 "Suppose that we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each resulting in success or failure, and with a probability of success $p.$ Let the random variable $X$ represent the number of Bernoulli trials *until the first success*.\n\nIt can be shown that $X$ follows a **geometric distribution** and has the following probability mass function:\n\n![](images/13/13d0eefea25458f7778607118a7673506900d72ee5b8a41826378c8737317e9e.svg)\n\nLet's break this formula down a bit:\n\n- The factor $(1-p)^{x-1}$ represents the probability of getting exactly $(x-1)$ failures, each with probability $(1-p).$\n\n- The factor ${\\color{purple}{p}}$ represents the probability of getting a single success.\n\n- The condition $x=1,2,3,\\ldots$ indicates that the sequence of Bernoulli trials terminates after a minimum of $1$ trial, and there is no maximum number of trials.\n\nIf a random variable $X$ follows a geometric distribution, we say it is a geometric random variable and  write \n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).\n$$" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168075 #edb/ref 1065 5055 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168075 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "fdc5cdcc-48e5-5c68-a8b0-d4a179a4f320" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168075 #edb/ref 1067 "The Probability Mass Function of a Geometric Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168108 #edb/ref 1064 "You might be wondering where the geometric distribution gets its name. The answer is that the probability mass function forms a geometric sequence! \n\nSuppose that $X\\sim \\textrm{Geom}(p).$ Then, the probability mass function for this geometric distribution is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}f(x) & =(1-p)^{x-1}p \\\\ f(x) & =p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\ {{f(x)}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{x}} & ={{p}_{⏟}}_{{a}_{1}}\\cdot ({{1-p}_{⏟}}_{r})^{x-1} \\\\ {a}_{x} & ={a}_{1}\\cdot {r}^{x-1}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSo, we see that the probability mass function can be interpreted as a geometric sequence where the first term is $a_1=p,$ and the common ratio is $r=1-p.$\n\nMoreover, notice that $f(x)$ satisfies all the criteria to be a probability mass function, namely\n\n1\\. $0 \\leq f(x) \\leq 1$ for all $x$ in $S$\n\n2\\. $\\displaystyle \\sum\\limits_{x \\in S} f(x) = 1$\n\nCriterion 1 is clearly true because $0 \\leq p \\leq 1.$ \n\nTo show that criterion 2 is true, we simply need to calculate the sum of this infinite geometric series, as follows:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{\\sum }_{x\\in S}f(x) & ={\\sum }_{x=1}^{\\infty }p(1-p)^{x-1} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{1-(1-p)} \\\\  & =\\frac{p}{p} \\\\  & =1\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, $f(x)$ is indeed a probability distribution." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168108 #edb/ref 1065 5365 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168108 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "0ac9b8c4-e381-5d24-b7f2-6e0cf660a14a" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168108 #edb/ref 1067 "Showing That a Geometric Distribution Is a Probability Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168131 #edb/ref 1064 "A **Bernoulli trial** is a random experiment whose outcome can be interpreted as either \"success\" or \"failure.\" \n\nFor example, suppose we throw a die and define a \"success\" as the event of getting a six and a \"failure\" as getting any other number. Then, the throw of a die can be thought of as a Bernoulli trial with the following probabilities:\n\n$$\nP(\\textrm{six}) = \\dfrac16, \\qquad P(\\textrm{not six}) = \\dfrac56\n$$\n\nNow suppose that a fair die is repeatedly thrown *until* we get a six. What is the probability that we need to throw the die precisely ${\\color{blue}{3}}$ times to get our first six?\n\nFirst, notice that each throw of the die is a Bernoulli trial. And since we throw the die repeatedly until we get a six, we have a *sequence* of Bernoulli trials. \n\nLet $X$ be the number of times the die is thrown until we get a six. We wish to compute $P(X = {\\color{blue}{3}}).$ \n\nTo get our first six on the *third* throw, we need to throw \"not a six\" on the *first two* throws, followed by a \"six\" on the *final* throw. Assuming that each throw of the die is independent, by the multiplication law for independent events, we have\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =P(\\text{not six})\\cdot P(\\text{not six})\\cdot P(\\text{six}) \\\\  & =\\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{5}{6}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{216}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nThe random variable $X$ in this example follows a so-called **geometric distribution**. Let's now discuss geometric distributions a little more generally." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168131 #edb/ref 1065 6168 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168131 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "3201acc8-7bbc-59e6-9a50-f9c5f19899e7" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168131 #edb/ref 1067 "Introduction" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168132 #edb/ref 1064 "Let $X$ be the number of times a fair die is thrown until we get a six. Since we have a sequence of Bernoulli trials, each with a probability of success $p=\\dfrac16,$ the number of throws until the first six can be modeled as a geometric random variable, and we write\n\n$$\nX\\sim \\textrm{Geom}\\left(\\dfrac16\\right).\n$$\n\nTherefore, the probability of getting our first six on the $\\color{blue}3$rd roll is\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}P(X=3) & =f(3) \\\\  & ={(1-\\frac{1}{6})}^{3-1}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & ={(\\frac{5}{6})}^{2}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{36}\\cdot \\frac{1}{6} \\\\  & =\\frac{25}{216}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwhich agrees with our previous result." #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168132 #edb/ref 1065 6169 #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168132 #edb/ref 1066 #uuid "95415f67-f3f8-5c48-9178-812ddf3830e1" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186168132 #edb/ref 1067 "Applying the Probability Mass Function of a Geometric Distribution" #edb/ref 17592186045417] [17592186428995 #edb/ref 1030 1.0 #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428995 #edb/ref 1032 "Initial Codex estimate from captured target-band crossing frequency. Selected account foundations: 1 rises / 1 eligible passed source activities = 1.000000; nearest selected fraction = 1. Crossing activity numbers [1]; waits [1]. Separate account estimates: foundations=1 (1/1). Selection: most observations, then lower weight, then account name; no averaging. Single-activity estimates included. Intervening target updates: False. Crossing frequency is the chosen practical estimate of encompassing coverage; a multi-band rise counts once. This does not interpret color steps as physical repetition units. Method: encompassing-report.md. Evidence: reference/mathacademy/encompassing-analysis-2026-10-11/frequency-fractions/foundations-frequency-estimates.json, pair 3284->1290. All account evidence retained in the prepared import's selected-relationships.json." #edb/ref 17592186045436] [17592186428995 #edb/ref 1036 #edb/ref 17592186048368 #edb/ref 17592186045436]}
